export const bodyHtml = "\n<p>A home office desk is judged by hours, not by photos. The best setups are the ones where the monitor, keyboard and chair stay put for a full day without you adjusting something every hour.</p>\n<p>These ideas are organised by the kind of work you do, with measurements so you can check them against your own desk.</p>\n<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> For a single monitor, a desk about 48 inches wide is comfortable, while dual monitors and a laptop do better with 60 inches or more. Put the screen's top near eye level, route cables to one point, and keep the second display or laptop off the main focus area.</p>\n<h2 id=\"setups-by-work-type\">Setups by work type</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Work type</th><th>Desk width to look for</th><th>Core setup</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Writing and email</td><td>About 40 to 48 in</td><td>Laptop on a stand, external keyboard</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Single 27 inch monitor</td><td>About 48 to 55 in</td><td>Monitor on an arm, compact keyboard</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dual monitors</td><td>About 60 in or more</td><td>Two arms, power strip under the desk</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Calls and meetings</td><td>About 48 in</td><td>Camera at eye level, soft side light</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p>These widths are planning ranges, not rules. A 27 inch 16:9 monitor is about 23.5 inches wide (calculated from the diagonal), so two of them need around 47 inches before stands and bezels.</p>\n<h2 id=\"monitor-and-arm\">Monitor placement and arms</h2>\n<p>A monitor arm clears the desk surface and lets you set the height exactly. Check that the desk edge can take a clamp, since hollow or thin tops can be a problem. Our <a href=\"/guide/best-monitor-arms\">monitor arm guide</a> lists mounts, and <a href=\"/guide/monitor-arm-hollow-core-desk\">monitor arms on hollow-core desks</a> covers the edge case.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lighting-and-calls\">Lighting and video calls</h2>\n<p>Keep the main light in front of or beside you, not behind you. A monitor light bar adds light on the desk without glare on the screen, and a webcam at eye level is more flattering than one pointing up from a laptop. See <a href=\"/guide/best-monitor-light-bars\">monitor light bars</a> for options.</p>\n<h2 id=\"storage-and-cables\">Storage and cable routing</h2>\n<p>Keep what you use daily within arm's reach and put the rest in a drawer or shelf. Group cables to one power strip fastened under the desk, and use a tray for adapters. The <a href=\"/guide/best-under-desk-cable-trays\">under-desk cable trays</a> guide shows ways to do that without drilling.</p>\n<h2 id=\"standing-and-sitting\">Add standing time if you can</h2>\n<p>If the desk does not raise, a converter sits on top and gives you a sit-stand option. Browse <a href=\"/guide/best-standing-desk-converters\">standing desk converters</a> and check desk depth, since converters need space behind the keyboard.</p>\n<h2 id=\"faq\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How should I set up a home office desk?</h3>\n<p>Set the chair first, then the monitor at about eye level, then keyboard and mouse, light and cables. Match desk width to your equipment.</p>\n<h3>How wide a desk do I need for a home office?</h3>\n<p>About 48 inches suits a single monitor, and 60 inches or more suits dual monitors with a laptop.</p>\n<h3>Where should the monitor sit on a home office desk?</h3>\n<p>Directly in front of you, an arm's length away, with the top near eye level. Place a second screen at an angle.</p>\n<h3>Is a monitor arm worth it?</h3>\n<p>It frees desk space and lets you set the height precisely, provided your desk edge accepts a clamp.</p>\n<h3>How do I hide cables in a home office?</h3>\n<p>Plug everything into one power strip, mount it under the desk, and use a tray or sleeve for the visible runs.</p>\n<h3>What lighting is best for a home office?</h3>\n<p>Soft light from the side or front, plus a task lamp. Avoid a bright window directly behind the screen.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-scope\">Sources and scope</h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.csuci.edu/publicsafety/ehs/200320-ci-workstation-setup-checklist.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CSU Channel Islands workstation checklist</a>: screen height and arm's length distances</li><li><a href=\"https://www.aut.ac.nz/__data/assets/pdf_file/0004/782680/Setting-up-your-workstation-tip-sheet.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AUT workstation tip sheet</a>: chair and reach basics for a full day</li><li><a href=\"https://www.obsbot.com/blog/work-from-home/ergonomic-desk-setup\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OBSBOT ergonomic desk setup</a>: remote work posture background</li></ul>\n<p>Desk widths are planning ranges and panel widths are calculated from screen diagonals. If you have a health condition, get advice from a qualified professional.</p>";
